// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    The Narrator
// @category Narrative Devices / Point of View
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Narrator
// @domain   Narrative
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): the "helpful invisible man that fills in the blanks of the story" — a
// voice that may sit inside the story or wholly external to it, and that ALWAYS breaks the fourth
// wall, explicitly addressing the audience to tell them the tale. FRONTIER: where Frame Story
// nests two diegeses and Unreliable Narrator weaponizes a non_canon plane, The Narrator names the
// bare structural fact underneath both — a TELLER on a level ABOVE the story addressing the
// audience plane OUTSIDE it. The narrator's own plane is canon and reliable here (cf. the
// Infallible Narrator); his telling is a crossing DOWN into the tale and OUT to the audience. He
// may be omniscient, a character looking back, or intrusive — the level geometry is the same.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"            // revelation, memory — what narration hands the audience

assoc the_narrator -> [
  frame_story         : "the bare voice beneath a",
  unreliable_narrator : "reliable here; contrast the",
  revelation          : "doles out, withholds, a",
  memory              : "often relates from, a"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===

imply TheNarrator -> [Narration, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: when a teller on the framing plane addresses the audience to relate the tale below,
// that act of telling IS Narration (tags the event). The narrator breaks the fourth wall by
// construction — the address reaches OUT to the audience while the tale sits one level DOWN.
rule The_Narrator {
  when:
    evt $tell [&Relates(agent=$teller, tale=$story)]
  then:
    $tell [+Narration]
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: A Series of Unfortunate Events — Lemony Snicket (children's novels, 1999) ===
// Lemony Snicket is a distinct character on his own plane, external to the Baudelaire orphans'
// misfortunes, who intrudes to define words, warn the reader away, and mourn — an omniscient,
// reliable, intrusive Narrator. Three planes, flat and ambient: `book` (the framing voice's plane,
// where Snicket writes), `book.tale` (the Baudelaire story he relates), and `audience` (the reader
// he keeps addressing — the fourth wall he steps through). Every plane change is a loud crossing.

// ===== the narrator's plane — Lemony Snicket at his desk, looking back =====
<<book : teller=lemony>>
char lemony [+Narrator] [+Observer]
lemony [~Mourns(target=<<book.tale|beatrice>>)]   // his grief reaches DOWN into the tale he tells

// ===== the tale he relates — the Baudelaire orphans, one level down =====
<<book.tale : teller=lemony>>
char violet [+Protagonist]
char olaf   [+Antagonist]
violet -- olaf : "the guardian who hunts the fortune"

// ===== the reader — the audience plane Snicket steps OUT to address =====
<<audience>>
char reader [+Bystander]

// ===== reopen the narrator's plane — the act of telling =====
<<book : teller=lemony>>
scene the_telling {
  beat 1 {
    // Snicket relates the tale below AND warns the reader above — the address that breaks the wall.
    evt recount [&Relates(agent=lemony, tale=<<book.tale|violet>>)]
    lemony [&Addresses(target=<<audience|reader>>)]
    // The_Narrator -> recount [+Narration]
  }
}
